abstract: >-
  The Thiele Machine is an abstract model of computation. A priced record
  system has states, moves, a step, a price per move, a reading into an
  ordered set, and a toll: a move that carries the reading somewhere not at
  or below where it was costs at least one. A Thiele machine is one whose
  reading some run raises from a designated start and no move lowers; a
  flat reading obeys the toll and is a shadow, not a member. The monograph develops retained
  distinctions, merges or fresh states, observation windows, and the room
  needed to preserve recoverable histories. Noninterference is an
  additional restriction where stated. This edition pairs the reviewed
  452-page monograph, which carries the arguments, with the matching
  repository snapshot, which also carries a formal check of the same
  statements. The work also treats threshold readings of growing
  records and the recursion theorem under its stated representation
  assumptions. The RFC 9162 discussion concerns a specified protocol
  fragment; its conclusions depend on the observer model. The cost-framework
  comparisons are correspondences under stated premises, not full embeddings.
  Physical interpretations require their stated physical premises; the
  physical interpretation remains a thesis, and the pointer criterion is a thesis.
  No surveyed external consequence satisfied all four novelty and applicability criteria.
  The software is licensed Apache-2.0. The monograph and documentation are
  licensed CC-BY-SA-4.0.
